{"ts": "2026-10-30T08:04:14.857+00:00", "method": "POST", "path": "/get", "status": 200, "duration_ms": 2109.35, "body_chars": 2547, "user_id": "u_0a36409773c9edba0fe0df291982b5aa9e279ed5ed1911805220fff814b239c2", "query": "A courtroom demonstration includes someone miming the act of unfastening a chain and opening a door. Given the described apartment layout and the testimony about which door was chain-locked, what is the most defensible inference about what the demonstration was meant to establish?\n\nA. That a bedroom door inside the apartment was chain-locked, limiting movement between the bedrooms and the hall.\nB. That the apartment’s front door to the stairway was secured by a chain, making immediate entry or exit through the hall contingent on undoing that chain.\nC. That the demonstrator was showing how the apartment’s front door opened into the interior hall of the apartment, emphasizing movement from room to room rather than establishing that the hall-and-stairway door itself was chain-locked.\nD. That the bathroom door was chain-locked, suggesting the witness was demonstrating confinement within the bathroom rather than the apartment.\nE. That the chain lock was on a kitchen door, implying access to the stairway required passing through the kitchen first.\nF. That the demonstrator was illustrating that the door leading to the outside hall and stairway was left unchained, so any delay at the threshold would have come from hesitation or fumbling rather than the need to unfasten a chain.\n\nPlease provide the option corresponding to the only correct answer, enclosed in parentheses, e.g., (X).", "top_k": 100, "body": "{\"query\":\"A courtroom demonstration includes someone miming the act of unfastening a chain and opening a door. Given the described apartment layout and the testimony about which door was chain-locked, what is the most defensible inference about what the demonstration was meant to establish?\\n\\nA. That a bedroom door inside the apartment was chain-locked, limiting movement between the bedrooms and the hall.\\nB. That the apartment’s front door to the stairway was secured by a chain, making immediate entry or exit through the hall contingent on undoing that chain.\\nC. That the demonstrator was showing how the apartment’s front door opened into the interior hall of the apartment, emphasizing movement from room to room rather than establishing that the hall-and-stairway door itself was chain-locked.\\nD. That the bathroom door was chain-locked, suggesting the witness was demonstrating confinement within the bathroom rather than the apartment.\\nE. That the chain lock was on a kitchen door, implying access to the stairway required passing through the kitchen first.\\nF. That the demonstrator was illustrating that the door leading to the outside hall and stairway was left unchained, so any delay at the threshold would have come from hesitation or fumbling rather than the need to unfasten a chain.\\n\\nPlease provide the option corresponding to the only correct answer, enclosed in parentheses, e.g., (X).\",\"user_id\":\"u_0a36409773c9edba0fe0df291982b5aa9e279ed5ed1911805220fff814b239c2\",\"top_k\":100,\"options\":[\"A. That a bedroom door inside the apartment was chain-locked, limiting movement between the bedrooms and the hall.\",\"B. That the apartment’s front door to the stairway was secured by a chain, making immediate entry or exit through the hall contingent on undoing that chain.\",\"C. That the demonstrator was showing how the apartment’s front door opened into the interior hall of the apartment, emphasizing movement from room to room rather than establishing that the hall-and-stairway door itself was chain-locked.\",\"D. That the bathroom door was chain-locked, suggesting the witness was demonstrating confinement within the bathroom rather than the apartment.\",\"E. That the chain lock was on a kitchen door, implying access to the stairway required passing through the kitchen first.\",\"F. That the demonstrator was illustrating that the door leading to the outside hall and stairway was left unchained, so any delay at the threshold would have come from hesitation or fumbling rather than the need to unfasten a chain.\"]}"}
{"ts": "2026-10-30T08:04:15.250+00:00", "method": "POST", "path": "/get", "status": 200, "duration_ms": 2498.48, "body_chars": 1328, "user_id": "u_314239a36dd4912e55d3a38be382bf7d9c573fd6807419e906ca292d695d1f27", "query": "What are some ways to make my weekend reading time feel more relaxing and immersive?", "top_k": 100, "body": "{\"query\":\"What are some ways to make my weekend reading time feel more relaxing and immersive?\",\"user_id\":\"u_314239a36dd4912e55d3a38be382bf7d9c573fd6807419e906ca292d695d1f27\",\"top_k\":100,\"options\":[\"Settle in with your favorite printed book in a cozy nook, maybe near a sunny window or with a soft lamp, and add a warm drink to sip on. Consider creating a small ritual, like lighting a candle before you open the pages, to make the time feel more special and immersive.\",\"Settle in with your favorite e-book on a tablet in a cozy nook, maybe near a sunny window or with a soft lamp, and add a warm drink to sip on. Consider creating a small ritual, like lighting a candle before you start swiping through the pages, to make the time feel more special and immersive.\",\"Settle in with something you enjoy reading in a comfortable spot, maybe near a sunny window or with a soft lamp, and add a warm drink to sip on. Consider creating a small ritual, like lighting a candle before you begin, to make the time feel more special and immersive.\",\"Settle in with your favorite audiobook playing in the background, perhaps while sitting in your favorite chair, and add a warm drink to sip on. Consider creating a small ritual, like lighting a candle before you put on your headphones, to make the time feel more special and immersive.\"]}"}
{"ts": "2026-10-30T08:04:15.263+00:00", "method": "POST", "path": "/get", "status": 200, "duration_ms": 2515.06, "body_chars": 2386, "user_id": "u_0a36409773c9edba0fe0df291982b5aa9e279ed5ed1911805220fff814b239c2", "query": "What does the camera’s movement and the crowding into the elevator most strongly imply about how the scene is framing the setting?\n\nA. It is signaling that the scene is shifting away from court activity toward administrative offices, with the elevator serving as a staff-only passage.\nB. It is establishing the courthouse as a busy, multi-courtroom hub, but with the elevator functioning mainly as the primary passage to administrative floors rather than to courtrooms.\nC. It is emphasizing a confined, private confrontation about to happen inside a single elevator, rather than the building’s broader function.\nD. It is establishing the courthouse as a busy, multi-courtroom hub where people are being funneled from a public lobby into more specialized spaces.\nE. It is indicating that the main action will occur outside the courthouse, using the interior only as a brief transition.\nF. It is establishing the courthouse as a hectic, multi-courtroom hub where the camera lingers on one elevator because it’s repeatedly delayed by people squeezing in, implying a bottlenecked access point that’s central to navigating the building.\n\nPlease provide the option corresponding to the only correct answer, enclosed in parentheses, e.g., (X).", "top_k": 100, "body": "{\"query\":\"What does the camera’s movement and the crowding into the elevator most strongly imply about how the scene is framing the setting?\\n\\nA. It is signaling that the scene is shifting away from court activity toward administrative offices, with the elevator serving as a staff-only passage.\\nB. It is establishing the courthouse as a busy, multi-courtroom hub, but with the elevator functioning mainly as the primary passage to administrative floors rather than to courtrooms.\\nC. It is emphasizing a confined, private confrontation about to happen inside a single elevator, rather than the building’s broader function.\\nD. It is establishing the courthouse as a busy, multi-courtroom hub where people are being funneled from a public lobby into more specialized spaces.\\nE. It is indicating that the main action will occur outside the courthouse, using the interior only as a brief transition.\\nF. It is establishing the courthouse as a hectic, multi-courtroom hub where the camera lingers on one elevator because it’s repeatedly delayed by people squeezing in, implying a bottlenecked access point that’s central to navigating the building.\\n\\nPlease provide the option corresponding to the only correct answer, enclosed in parentheses, e.g., (X).\",\"user_id\":\"u_0a36409773c9edba0fe0df291982b5aa9e279ed5ed1911805220fff814b239c2\",\"top_k\":100,\"options\":[\"A. It is signaling that the scene is shifting away from court activity toward administrative offices, with the elevator serving as a staff-only passage.\",\"B. It is establishing the courthouse as a busy, multi-courtroom hub, but with the elevator functioning mainly as the primary passage to administrative floors rather than to courtrooms.\",\"C. It is emphasizing a confined, private confrontation about to happen inside a single elevator, rather than the building’s broader function.\",\"D. It is establishing the courthouse as a busy, multi-courtroom hub where people are being funneled from a public lobby into more specialized spaces.\",\"E. It is indicating that the main action will occur outside the courthouse, using the interior only as a brief transition.\",\"F. It is establishing the courthouse as a hectic, multi-courtroom hub where the camera lingers on one elevator because it’s repeatedly delayed by people squeezing in, implying a bottlenecked access point that’s central to navigating the building.\"]}"}
