{"ts": "2026-10-30T09:45:10.936+00:00", "method": "POST", "path": "/get", "status": 200, "duration_ms": 478.64, "body_chars": 1282, "user_id": "u_fed07021676f4c47fb623904c6061004bb5dcd3e5507cec875b5ddc5c06e9cff", "query": "NTSB preliminary excerpt:\nAt 0816 local, an experimental amateur-built, two-seat, low-wing airplane departed RWY 27 on a local flight. Surface temperature was 0–1°C with light frost reported on the upper wing surfaces during preflight. The pilot stated he performed a run-up, set flaps to 15°, and commenced takeoff at near gross weight. At approximately 200 ft AGL, with the IAS indicating 63 KIAS (published clean stall speed 60 KIAS), he retracted the flaps to 0°. ADS-B data showed a brief gust from 290° at 18 kt within 5 s of flap retraction. Witnesses observed a buffet followed by a sudden left-wing drop and descending turn. Postaccident examination found no engine or control anomalies. A thin, granular frost layer was present on portions of both wings, and a section of trailing-edge protective tape (about 18 inches) was partially detached on the left wing outboard of midspan. Fuel was uncontaminated; control continuity was confirmed.\n\nAnalyze this NTSB preliminary report excerpt regarding an experimental aircraft's sudden loss of lift. Parse the application of the Kutta-Joukowski theorem to evaluate the circulation failure at the critical boundaries.", "top_k": 100, "body": "{\"query\":\"NTSB preliminary excerpt:\\nAt 0816 local, an experimental amateur-built, two-seat, low-wing airplane departed RWY 27 on a local flight. Surface temperature was 0–1°C with light frost reported on the upper wing surfaces during preflight. The pilot stated he performed a run-up, set flaps to 15°, and commenced takeoff at near gross weight. At approximately 200 ft AGL, with the IAS indicating 63 KIAS (published clean stall speed 60 KIAS), he retracted the flaps to 0°. ADS-B data showed a brief gust from 290° at 18 kt within 5 s of flap retraction. Witnesses observed a buffet followed by a sudden left-wing drop and descending turn. Postaccident examination found no engine or control anomalies. A thin, granular frost layer was present on portions of both wings, and a section of trailing-edge protective tape (about 18 inches) was partially detached on the left wing outboard of midspan. Fuel was uncontaminated; control continuity was confirmed.\\n\\nAnalyze this NTSB preliminary report excerpt regarding an experimental aircraft's sudden loss of lift. Parse the application of the Kutta-Joukowski theorem to evaluate the circulation failure at the critical boundaries.\",\"user_id\":\"u_fed07021676f4c47fb623904c6061004bb5dcd3e5507cec875b5ddc5c06e9cff\",\"top_k\":100}"}
